From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 03:36:18 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] feat(interaction): preserve native held item use

Native Bedrock 1.26.60.5 keeps the first predicted trapdoor facing, half
and world hit during held placement. Retain that state across materials,
item changes, correction and stack exhaustion. Apply the snap only to
trapdoors; ordinary blocks share the retained placement path below,
and empty hands restore interaction. Returning to a trapdoor restores its snap until release.
Preserve independent water, power and material properties. Separate
trapdoors and the original support remain eligible targets.

Carry physical use lifetime and sequence-owned prediction through direct
and ViaProxy sessions before the concluding Java packet. Negotiate
format 3 for air admission, with format 2 retained for older cores.
Keep use-target selection separate from the visible Java raycast. Retain
native cursor coordinates outside a later target cube. Select native outline/occlusion
from the active Bedrock connection rather than the local Java protocol.
Native bottom-face hits are about 0.8175, including translated proxy joins.

Keep two clock lifetimes: air use has a strict 200 ms connection deadline;
held placement waits strictly more than 300 ms before an established
line exists, then strictly more than 200 ms after each line success.
Failed placement repeats do not restart their delay. Retry from input
frames after camera movement, before the next tick publishes rotation,
and suppress Java's parallel four-tick path. Native frame quantization
rules out a guessed five-tick delay. Release/screens retire the held mode
and placement state while retaining the air deadline. Connection
replacement resets both. Missing optional block channels clear only
placement state, preserving an active air hold and its deadline.

Retain the interaction mode across item changes. Current native tracing
starts with fresh flags 176 then keeps held air-only flag 16 after selecting
a trapdoor. Only the initial call visits block use; held frames call air
use after the deadline. Unattached stone and late trapdoor controls agree.
The reverse trapdoor-to-food control keeps simulation block uses and
consumes no food. A new press after release can place the selected block.

Choose the initial food route from transported FOOD and the local
consumable predicate, including custom paper carriers. Set the air deadline
before main-hand food use even when hunger refuses it. Initial refusal
retains untimed block mode, rather than acquiring the air route. An owned air hold
skips block/entity dispatch and offhand fallback; newly selected items
share its air deadline. A placement hold cannot fall through to eating.
Canonical consumption/completion remain server-owned. Initial block-item
air use retains untimed block mode. Native item predicates select the
other initial air-use routes as described below.

The installed baseline places four Stone blocks after a food hold and
consumes six foods after a trapdoor hold, unlike native. Input-state tests
cover both directions, hand/target routing, variable frames, release,
connection reset, clock boundaries and rollover. Native and installed
captures establish gameplay behavior separately from model tests. Other
use families, exact packet phases, complete use cycles and the original
intermittent food report remain open.

Current 1.26.60.5/protocol 2225 refused-food traces retain held flags 17
with or without an initial block target. Accepted food retains flags 16
even after hunger fills. The refused route writes a 200 ms air deadline
but bypasses it for block dispatch. A Stone selected 110 ms
after refused bread can place immediately. Successful ordinary block
uses start the same 300 ms placement delay. Separate block mode ownership
from its active delay, and distinguish frame continuation from fresh
physical calls without allocating callbacks on the input path.

Retain a build direction when adjacent placement attempts establish a
line. Test the ray segment against the next cell, then dispatch the
retained support and face without replacing the visible hit or its world
location. Advance the pending cell only on a successful local placement.
Clear it with the physical hold and the existing connection/screen owner.

Original 1.26.60.5/protocol 2225 helper14376b8d0 clips the current ray
against the retained next-cell AABB. Build14376bb80 records adjacent
integer deltas and advances the cell only when its build succeeds. The
clip helper142d345c0 uses float segment/box SAT with half extents and a
0.0001 cross-axis tolerance. Preserve that arithmetic rather than using
Java's double ray intersection or a same-face rule.

Fresh/refused Stone native controls place two blocks, then reject the
new top hit outside the pending cell. Re-aiming onto the floor crosses
that cell and dispatches the old north face with the original world hit,
including a negative relative Z cursor. The third placement succeeds;
further player-collision failures keep the next cell pending. Returning
to the old aim stops attempts, and release allows fresh top placement.
Regression fixtures retain those native float coordinates, pending-cell
rejection, advancement, all six axes and release behavior. Moving actors,
entity/air targets and broader mixed-use state still need comparisons.

Read the build-delay constants from this same executable:300.0 at
14f10c440,200.0 at14efc03a8. Once line mode is active the latter wins,
and the clock comparison rejects equality. Keep this independent from
the separate air-use deadline and preserve failed-repeat behavior.

Construct each box maximum by adding one to its already converted float
minimum. Converting an incremented integer instead adds a spurious extent
when the native cell collapses near float precision limits. A concrete
large-coordinate ray regression fails before that arithmetic correction.
The same executable's underlying build callback143771570 retains its
first-success flag across failures, matching the path owner's lifetime.

Preserve the last successful build position on ordinary rejection. The
native wrapper records a rejected target only at its upper height limit.
Keep that exception separate from the first-success flag and pending-cell
advancement. Regressions cover a failed distant attempt before returning
to the original line, and the upper-height history branch. Live height
limits and broader mixed-use cases remain separate validation work.

Native first-rejected Stone and trapdoor controls invoke ordinary air use
once, write the strict 200 ms deadline, then keep flags 17 and untimed
block continuation. Java returns before that air phase on FAIL. Permit
its initial main-hand block fallback, acquire block mode from the air
phase, and prevent subsequent air/offhand fallback. The same native
unattached-Stone control selects this block mode without a block target.
Remove the old trapdoor-only first-rejection delay.

Record the client air deadline for the first successful placement only
when an item remains and its air phase passes the previous deadline. Core patch 0091 already emits that air transaction. Do not
send another from the add-on. Held placement success must not refresh
this deadline. Native last-block placement and trapdoor activation write
no air deadline; leave those phases separate. Current Bedrock 1.26.60.5 tracing
observes setter 1465c0050 from 14658623d before air use 14376e0c0,
plus repeat 14376b8d0 and the resulting protocol 2225 transactions.

Input regressions cover initial rejection, immediate frame continuation,
one air phase, later success, first-placement deadline ownership, held
success, last-stack exclusion and release into food. Native capture frame
cadence differs from Java, so retry counts are not equality assertions.
Rapid input edge sampling and broader item dispatch remain open.

Fresh block dispatch does not wait for the air clock. Native tracing of
1.26.60.5 observes a fresh flags 176 callback and failed block use about
27 ms before the preceding air deadline expires. It emits no new air
transaction. Remove the whole-call gate; admit air separately. If the
first known food/block air phase is suppressed, retain untimed block
continuation instead of starting air use after the deadline on that hold.

A fresh physical call clears the previous mode, placement path and core
session before starting its new dispatch. This also handles a release
and re-press between sampled frames. Retain the connection air deadline.
A first successful placement records air admission with its prediction
before the concluding Java use-on packet. Core can then suppress only
its extra air phase. Keep the legacy format/channel fallback for older
cores, whose unconditional first-success air behavior remains limited.
Regression cases cover suppressed fresh air, independent block success,
unchanged air deadlines and release into a new admitted food hold.

Mark fresh calls at the consumed-click call site in handleKeybinds,
separately from Java tick fallback and the frame owner. A legacy tick
retry must not reset the native physical lifetime or acquire another
Player_Input trigger. Retain untimed block continuation for empty hands,
including a fresh press after the last placed block. Preserve an already
owned air route. Installed rapid last-stack controls exposed the extra
fresh trigger before this distinction.

Only an owned air or block mode can retry from frames. The earlier
holding-food shortcut could preempt Java's queued fresh click, then
reset its new air hold when the click was consumed on the next tick.
The old whole-call clock gate hid that duplicate dispatch. Remove the
shortcut now that initial air admission selects its continuation mode.
A boundary regression rejects frame use before physical admission, then
checks the first eligible frames of the admitted hold. Installed food-to-
block controls expose the duplicate before this ownership correction.

Preserve fresh entity admission behind the air deadline. The same native
common-input source gates its entity branch with the clock while fresh
block dispatch uses flags 0x21 independently. Suppress only the entity
target before admission, allowing the ordinary air predicate to select
the continuation. A target-boundary regression keeps block hits eligible
without opening entity or air admission early. Held entity dispatch and
broader actor handlers remain separate comparison work.

For item context that supplies native predicates, record the strict
200 ms air deadline before every admitted main-hand air callback. Select
the initial continuation after that callback from active use or retained
stack count and the native item predicate. Keep an existing air route
across item changes and failed callbacks. A fresh clock rejection retains
block continuation and must not start air use later during the same hold.

Original Bedrock1.26.60.5 instruction controls establish this separation:
32 outcome combinations and six original Ender Pearl vtable cases keep
the route independent of the ordinary air callback result. ComponentItem
predicates check throwable and legacy-trigger-on-use components; use core
metadata so Java carrier classes do not choose the mode. Retain the prior
food admission snapshot when Java completes zero-duration food inside
its callback. Older proxy cores without the field retain the previous
input fallback. Full physical timing, real projectile cooldowns, Education
predicates and matching installed direct/proxy controls remain open.

Capture actual local stack consumption before ordinary item use and send
its result after prediction, before the concluding Java packet. Match
the prediction sequence in core. Cooldown refusal still sends air use
but must not invent a legacy inventory debit. Compare native definition
identity as well as quantity, and retain the existing core fallback when
the optional consumption channel is absent.

Original Bedrock 1.26.60.5 client callbacks record one pearl debit for a
successful use and no inventory action for a cooldown-refused use. Java
also sends a packet when its local cooldown skips item use. Reporting
its actual stack change lets core reproduce the native action envelope
and receive authority for late server acceptance. Keep this separate
from result-independent air-route ownership and the strict 200 ms clock.
